        "id": 121069,
        "name": "Rabit",
        "username": "Rabit",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/rabit/{size}/7330_2.png",
        "created_at": "2014-11-21T14:53:47.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>Nah, you're not misremembering it. (I'm not a good judge of crazy, so I'll leave that to others.  <img src=\"//forums.greaterthangames.com/images/emoji/apple/wink.png?v=9\" title=\":wink:\" class=\"emoji\" alt=\":wink:\"> )</p>\n<p>Christopher has always (in the past) said you do as much as you can of an action. You can see this in all these examples of previous rulings.</p>\n<p>I can understand why Christopher ruled it differently here, though. This is a very different situation for one reason only: the cards revealed are then compared. In the past, we've never had the revealed cards matter to each other - each could be managed independent of the other. That's not an option here. So Christopher had to come up with something that makes sense.</p>\n<p>I do agree that it would be more consistent to say the single card revealed, compared to nothing, and - since it doesn't match - is discarded. But I can understand why this situation is different from past rulings.</p>",
        "post_number": 21,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T03:54:36.270Z",
